text:
Sam: quick question about the JS client and streaming responses — we're seeing partial JSON objects split across SSE/data chunks and i'm not sure what's the cleanest approach to reassemble + backpressure. any best-practices?

Lia: what's the client runtime? browser fetch + ReadableStream or node fetch/undici? details matter for backpressure.

Sam: browser first, but we want the library to behave sensibly in node too. currently using fetch + reader.read() loop with TextDecoder.

Mike: ok. high-level: accumulate bytes in a buffer, decode incrementally, split on your record delimiter (newline or double-newline), try to JSON.parse each complete chunk, and keep remainder for next read. also respect readableStream backpressure by awaiting reader.read() in a controlled loop.

Sam: example pseudocode would help — i'm tripping on where to do TextDecoder vs JSON parsing safely (partial multibyte utf-8 sequences).

Lia: use TextDecoder with {stream: true} to avoid breaking multibyte chars across chunks. pattern:

```
const decoder = new TextDecoder('utf-8');
let buffered = '';
const reader = body.getReader();
while(true){
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buffered += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  let parts = buffered.split('\n\n'); // or whatever delimiter your server uses
  buffered = parts.pop(); // last is incomplete
  for(const p of parts){
    try{ handleJSON(JSON.parse(p)); }catch(e){ /* handle parse error, maybe emit partial */ }
  }
}
if(buffered) handleJSON(JSON.parse(buffered));
```

Mike: that covers char boundaries. for SSE specifically, data frames typically end with "\n\n" so splitting on double-newline is fine if server follows that. if server streams tokens like partial JSON deltas (e.g., { "delta": "..." } pieces), you might need a higher-level framing protocol.

Jen: fwiw our backend often sends top-level JSON objects per event, but for chat completions we stream small JSON deltas that together form one logical message. for that case i keep an event-assembly step: collect deltas until an event "done": true flag then combine.

Sam: how do we avoid blocking the main thread when a large backlog of events arrives? we want to signal to the fetch stream to slow down or drop if consumer is slow.

Lia: in browsers, ReadableStream backpressure works if you pipe to a TransformStream that only pulls when downstream is ready. in simple reader loops you can await some consumer promise before continuing to read more chunks. something like:

```
let consumerPromise = Promise.resolve();
while(true){
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  const text = decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
  queue.push(text);
  // wait until consumer drains enough
  await consumerPromise;
  consumerPromise = maybeDrainQueue();
}
```

Mike: in node you have more explicit flow control with streams.pause()/resume() or by not calling read() until ready. for undici's fetch you still get a web-like body stream so same pattern applies.

Sam: any thoughts on dropping partial events vs buffering forever? we have tight memory constraints on some clients.

Jen: expose a maxBufferedBytes option. if buffered bytes exceed threshold, emit a 'backpressure' event and either drop oldest or reject future reads. make it configurable. default: 2MB.

Lia: also consider a maxEventCount and a policy enum: 'block'|'drop-oldest'|'drop-new'. document tradeoffs.

Sam: parsing errors — sometimes a chunk contains two events concatenated without expected delimiter (server bug). Should client attempt to recover?

Mike: try to be conservative: if parse fails, keep the raw string in a parse-error queue and emit a non-fatal error event with the raw text. avoid aggressive heuristics that can silently mutate data. include raw bytes in logs for debugging.

ci-bot: deploy-check: streaming-client PR #402 has failing unit tests for incremental decode — flagged for devex review :warning:.

Sam: noted, i'll push a PR update today addressing TextDecoder usage + add maxBufferedBytes. heads-up to reviewers: the core change is using decoder.decode(chunk, {stream:true}) and plumbing backpressure hooks.

Jen: happy to review — can you add a short integration test that streams split multibyte utf-8 characters across chunks? saved me once. :grin:

Sam: will do. thanks all. :thumbsup:
